{{Short description|Lacashian securities backed by projected refugee land rent}}

'''Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates''' were revenue-backed securities issued by the government of [[Lacashe]] in 2025 against rent expected from state-owned agricultural plots used by the [[Wolffrean refugee settlement programme]]. The certificates were commonly called rent futures, although they constituted the advance sale of future public revenue rather than standardized {{w|futures contract}}s.

Refugee households received agricultural leases with five-year rent holidays. Investors purchased certificates that entitled them to distributions from rent collected after the holidays expired. Government projections assumed that refugee admissions would continue, increasing both quarterly hosting grants from the [[Adonian Community]] and the number of future rent-bearing leases. The Ministry of Finance sold approximately I$1.25 billion in certificates during 2025.

Trading increased during late 2025 and early 2026, and the certificates reached an estimated secondary-market value of I$1.7 billion. Their prices fell by between 70 and 80 per cent after the [[Refugee Adjustment Act of 2026]] ended group-based admissions on 15 August. The collapse contributed to the [[2026 Lacashian bond crisis]].

== Background ==

From 2024, Lacashe settled most Wolffrean refugees in the northwestern agricultural districts. The government retained ownership of the land and granted each refugee household a lease with no rent due during its first five years. The plots were accompanied by trailer housing, agricultural subsidies and access to settlement infrastructure.

The programme was financed by AC grants paid at I$7,850 per registered refugee. Transfers rose to I$2.222 billion in 2025. Construction and subsidy costs also increased, and the government sought additional financing without immediately raising taxes or reducing the anti-inflation programmes.

The Ministry of Finance began treating future lease rent as a saleable public receivable. Most of the rent would not be collected until the large admission cohorts of 2024–2026 completed their five-year holidays between 2029 and 2031. The government expected continued admissions to enlarge the future rent base.

== Structure ==

The Ministry of Finance established a Settlement Revenue Trust to receive an assigned share of rent collected from programme plots. The trust issued certificates denominated in International dollars through a syndicate led by the [[Lacashe Commercial Bank]] and foreign financial institutions. Trading subsequently occurred through banks and over-the-counter securities dealers in Guledga and foreign financial centres.

The certificates transferred a share of the designated rent stream but did not transfer ownership of the land. The government remained the lessor and retained responsibility for assessment, collection, arrears and enforcement. Existing refugee leases were not modified by the certificates.

Returns accrued during the rent-free period. A reserve funded from AC grants supported administrative expenses. The legal documents did not guarantee a minimum level of rent, a number of future admissions or a fixed exchange value for rent collected in hanti. The certificates also lacked a general sovereign or AC guarantee.

Projected additions to the rent pool were calculated from admission scenarios. New refugees were expected to receive additional state plots and become rent-paying leaseholders five years later. These projections linked certificate valuations to the continuation of the Wolffrea War Refugee Act even though future admissions were not legally required.

== Rent schedule ==

The timing of the projected revenue followed the five-year holiday for each lease-allocation cohort.

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Projected beginning of rent collection
! Lease-allocation cohort
! Rent begins
|-
| 2024
| 2029
|-
| 2025
| 2030
|}

The first large rent collections were scheduled for 2029. Investors therefore valued the certificates principally through long-term population, rent, exchange-rate and collection assumptions.

== Issuance and market growth ==

The first certificates were placed during the second quarter of 2025. Approximately I$1.25 billion was sold during the year. Banks, investment funds and foreign investors purchased the securities for their projected yield and their perceived association with the state and the AC-funded settlement programme.

The market value of the outstanding certificates rose to approximately I$1.7 billion in early 2026. Continued refugee arrivals and AC grants were interpreted as evidence that the government would maintain the settlement programme. Certificates were also accepted as collateral by Lacashian banks, increasing their use in domestic lending.

The government applied the proceeds to essential-goods subsidies, utility transfers, construction and rural employment. The sale reduced the need for immediate taxation but assigned part of future land revenue to investors. Opposition deputies criticized the use of income that would not be collected for several years to finance current expenditure.

== Collapse ==

The Refugee Adjustment Act ended group-based admissions on 15 August 2026. The final admission-linked AC payment followed the registrations recorded through that date. No new leases were added under the settlement programme after repeal.

Certificate prices fell because the projected lease base stopped growing. The relocation assistance offered by the act also created uncertainty over whether existing leaseholders would remain until rent became payable. The depreciation of the hanti reduced the International-dollar value of domestic rent, while the fiscal position of the government weakened after the loss of AC transfers.

Prices declined by between 70 and 80 per cent during the following weeks. Some certificates traded below one quarter of their original issue value. Banks reduced their collateral values, investment funds recorded losses, and foreign investors sold both certificates and Lacashian sovereign bonds.

== Legal and political response ==

The Ministry of Finance maintained that the certificates disclosed the absence of guaranteed admissions, rent levels and AC support. Investors and opposition politicians argued that official projections had treated continued admissions as the central scenario and had not adequately described the risk of repeal.

The early-2026 sovereign bond issue and the earlier sale of certificates became subjects of demands for a National Assembly inquiry. Questions concerned the admission assumptions, the use of AC grants as a reserve, the valuation of delayed rent, and the treatment of certificate holdings by domestic banks.

Existing certificates remained legal claims on the assigned share of rent collected from surviving leases. No mass rent collection had begun as of November 2026, and their ultimate recovery value remained uncertain.

